.TH SYNC 9
.SH NAME
sync \- spinlocks, sleeping locks, rendezvous, read/write locks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:sync"
.PP
.nf
.B "acquire :: proc (l: ^Spinlock) -> Guard"
.B "release :: proc (l: ^Spinlock, g: Guard)"
.B "held :: proc (l: ^Spinlock) -> bool"
.B "can_sleep :: proc () -> bool"
.B "require_sleepable :: proc (reason: string)"
.PP
.B "mutex_lock :: proc (m: ^Mutex)"
.B "mutex_unlock :: proc (m: ^Mutex)"
.B "mutex_held :: proc (m: ^Mutex) -> bool"
.PP
.B "sleep :: proc (r: ^Rendez, cond: Condition, arg: rawptr = nil)"
.B "sleep_noted :: proc (r: ^Rendez, cond: Condition, arg: rawptr = nil) -> bool"
.B "sleep_for :: proc (r: ^Rendez, cond: Condition, arg: rawptr, ticks: u64) -> bool"
.B "delay :: proc (ticks: u64)"
.B "wakeup :: proc (r: ^Rendez) -> bool"
.B "wakeup_all :: proc (r: ^Rendez) -> int"
.PP
.B "rlock :: proc (l: ^RW_Lock)"
.B "runlock :: proc (l: ^RW_Lock)"
.B "wlock :: proc (l: ^RW_Lock)"
.B "wunlock :: proc (l: ^RW_Lock)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sync
provides two kinds of mutual exclusion and one kind of waiting, distinguished
by whether a thread may leave the CPU while it holds one.
.SS Spinlock
A
.B Spinlock
masks interrupts on the current core and, on more than one core, also
spins on a word the losing core cannot take.
.I Acquire
returns a
.B Guard
recording whether interrupts were on before the call;
.I release
restores that state, so a lock taken inside a handler that was already
masked does not turn interrupts back on.
The same core may take a
.B Spinlock
it already holds; a nesting count in
.B depth
lets only the outermost
.I release
clear the lock, which is what lets
.B heap.odin's
.B resize
call
.B alloc
while already holding the heap lock.
A
.B Spinlock
may be held anywhere and must be released within a few instructions.
It may never be held across a wait: a thread that leaves the CPU with
interrupts masked leaves them masked, because nothing else will unmask
them.
.PP
.B can_sleep
is how that rule is checked rather than remembered.
It reports false if the current core is inside any
.BR Spinlock ,
counted per core rather than per lock, or if the caller is a top half
running in interrupt context and so is not a thread that can be the one
put to sleep.
.I Rpc
in
.B kernel/vfs
calls it before sending a 9P message that might park, and refuses with
.B EDEADLK
rather than risk the hang.
.B require_sleepable
stops the machine, naming the reason, if the caller could not have
parked: the same hazard reaches a core that waits for another core's
answer while holding a spinlock the other core wants.
.SS Mutex
A
.B Mutex
parks the loser instead of masking interrupts, for a hold that spans a
whole 9P message rather than a few instructions.
.I Mutex_lock
calls
.B can_sleep
first and fails loudly if it is inside a spinlock.
.B mutex_unlock
with a thread queued does not simply clear the lock: it hands ownership
to the best-priority waiter and makes that thread runnable with nothing
left to check, which is a handoff rather than a retry.
Put back to first-come-first-served ordering instead of best-priority,
the tail latency among several session workers grows by an order of
magnitude, which is why the queue is served by priority and not by
arrival.
What a
.B Mutex
does not fix is priority inversion: a low-priority holder still delays a
high-priority waiter for as long as it holds the lock.
.SS Rendez
A
.B Rendez
is a place to wait for a condition to become true, Plan 9's
.B Rendez
by name.
.I Sleep
takes a
.B Condition
procedure rather than a queue handle, and runs it itself after the
caller is already registered on the queue, which closes the race between
testing a condition and being woken for it.
The condition runs with no lock of this package held, so it must not
allocate, must not log and must not take a sleeping lock; it may take a
spinlock, which is what lets a condition read a device's state under the
device's own lock.
.PP
.B wakeup
makes the best waiter runnable and reports whether it found one;
.B wakeup_all
does the same for every waiter.
Waking is a hint, not a promise: a third thread can make the condition
false again before the woken one actually runs, so
.I sleep
loops, re-testing the condition after every wake and parking again if it
is still false.
A caller only ever learns that the condition was true at the moment
.I sleep
returned.
A
.B wakeup
that finds nobody waiting does nothing and is not remembered, because
the condition itself is what carries the state; a thread that arrives
afterward runs the test, finds it true, and never parks.
.PP
.B sleep_noted
is
.B sleep
that also returns if a note arrives for the thread's process, reporting
false in that case so the caller can unwind toward whatever boundary
delivers
.BR EINTR .
A kernel thread is never noted, so for one this behaves exactly like
.BR sleep .
.B sleep_for
adds a tick deadline, reporting false if it expired first;
.I ticks
of zero is a poll that never parks.
.B delay
is the degenerate case: a
.B Rendez
on the caller's own stack that nothing else can name, ended only by the
clock.
Time throughout this package is in scheduler ticks, fed to it by the
timer interrupt; there is no finer resolution to promise.
.SS The sleep queue and deadlines
A waiting thread's node lives on its own stack for the length of the
wait.
A thread waiting with a deadline sits on two lists, the condition's
queue and a deadline list sorted soonest-first on insert; whichever
fires first unlinks the node from both before waking, so the second
trigger to arrive finds nothing left to do.
.SS RW_Lock
.B RW_Lock
is Plan 9's reader/writer lock, held across a walk that must see one
consistent member list from start to end.
.I Rlock
takes the lock immediately if there is no writer and nobody already
queued; otherwise it queues behind whoever is there, so a stream of
arriving readers cannot starve a queued writer.
.I Runlock
starts the writer at the head of the queue if the last reader just left
and one is waiting.
.I Wlock
takes the lock immediately if there are no readers and no writer;
otherwise it queues.
.I Wunlock
hands over to a writer at the head, or admits every reader at the head
of the queue together, up to the first writer.
The queue is served strictly in arrival order, not by priority: a
reader's or writer's position, not its priority, is the policy here.
The wake used to move a queued thread is
.B unpark
rather than
.BR ready ,
because a thread queued on this lock was doing exactly what the thread
ahead of it was doing, and that distinction belongs to the scheduler,
not the lock.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/sync
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sched (9),
.IR arch (9),
.IR vfs (9),
.IR mnt (9),
.IR rendezvous (2),
.IR semacquire (2)
.SH BUGS
A
.B Mutex
does not implement priority inheritance.
A low-priority holder can still delay a high-priority waiter for the
whole of its hold.
